Paragraph econd": {
    "total_score": 0.08879999999999999,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "Random access to data is a foundational operation in computer science, enabling efficient algorithms for search, selection, and exploration. However, this operation largely breaks down in mod- ern data systems where access is governed by ranking functions rather than static keys. In such systems, data items are ordered by a query-specific ,user-specific , ormachine-generated scoring function,1 often dynamically defined over high-dimensional feature spaces. Yet, accessing the items at arbitrary rank positions or within a rank window typically requires fully materializing or sequentially scanning the ranked list, using operations such as get-next . This limitation arises across a wide range of data-driven applica- tions, including database systems, interactive data exploration and analytics tools, information retrieval and recommendation systems, and machine learning pipelines [ 13,19,21,33,42,84]. Common scenarios include database queries on derived attributes[ 64,68], 1Also known as ranking functions.analytical exploration for business decisions [ 68], paged naviga- tion of search results [ 33], browsing ranked product lists in e- commerce [ 78], and scrolling through personalized feeds on social platforms [65]. In web and e-commerce applications, APIs commonly useoffset-based pagination . More generally, this pattern requires efficient retrieval of results at deeper ranks without scanning a"
    "citation_focus_text": "Random access to data is a foundational operation in computer science, enabling efficient algorithms for search, selection, and exploration. This limitation arises across a wide range of data-driven applica- tions, including database systems, interactive data exploration and analytics tools, information retrieval and recommendation systems, and machine learning pipelines [ 13,19,21,33,42,84]. Common scenarios include database queries on derived attributes[ 64,68], 1Also known as ranking functions.analytical exploration for business decisions [ 68], paged naviga- tion of search results [ 33], browsing ranked product lists in e- commerce [ 78], and scrolling through personalized feeds on social platforms [65]. In web and e-commerce applications, APIs commonly useoffset-based pagination . More generally, this pattern requires efficient retrieval of results at deeper ranks without scanning a"
  },
  "Paragraph 2": {
    "total_score": 0.0312,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "Direct access to conjunctive queries in database systems has been studied to directly access a specific position in the join re- sult, without fully materializing the join [ 14,23,42,93]. However, these works assume a prespecified ordering of the tuples (e.g., lex- icographic order on an attribute) and focus on skipping the join operation. As a result, such approaches are not suitable for settings where the ranking function is part of the input query.2 Contributions . In this paper, we formalize and study the Random- Access Ranked Retrieval (RAR ) problem and the Random-Access Similarity Search problem ( RAS) (Section 3). We show a reduction from RAS toRAR problem. Next, we propose an algorithm for RAR that uses computational geometric concepts of duality, arrange- ments, and levels of arr",
    "citation_focus_text": "Direct access to conjunctive queries in database systems has been studied to directly access a specific position in the join re- sult, without fully materializing the join [ 14,23,42,93]. However, these works assume a prespecified ordering of the tuples (e.g., lex- icographic order on an attribute) and focus on skipping the join operation. In this paper, we formalize and study the Random- Access Ranked Retrieval (RAR ) problem and the Random-Access Similarity Search problem ( RAS) (Section 3). Consequently, we propose several algorithms with linear space complexity even for a large number of attributes, based on the con- cept of\ud835\udf00-samples (Section 6). We start from the two-dimensional case and extend our algorithm to higher dimensions by combining it with the stripe range searching problem. Efficiently finding the tuples within a narrow stripe range is fundamental in the development of ou"
  }
}
